# ─── Required ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Anthropic API key — used server-side for all AI calls.
# Get yours at https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
#
# Important: an admin on your Anthropic Console account must enable web
# search first (Settings → Privacy → Web Search). Full Validation will
# fail at the research phase without it. Fast Check does not need it.
#
# Add to your shell profile (~/.zshrc, ~/.bashrc, ~/.bash_profile) or use
# a secrets manager (Doppler, 1Password CLI) so this file stays a template.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY}

# ─── Required in production ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Upstash Redis — distributed rate limiter. Without these, the limiter
# falls back to an in-memory store that resets on cold start AND is
# per-instance on serverless platforms, which means a single client can
# drain the Anthropic credit budget by issuing parallel requests across
# Vercel instances.
#
# In local dev the in-memory fallback is fine. In production it is not.
# Provision a free Upstash database at https://console.upstash.com/redis
# and copy the REST URL and token here.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=${UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL}
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=${UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN}

# ─── Recommended in production (cost-spike circuit breaker) ──────────────────

# Daily Anthropic-spend ceilings. The site is shared as a portfolio piece;
# per-IP rate limiting handles single-machine abuse but doesn't stop viral
# distribution or VPN-rotation. The spend ledger (lib/spend-ledger.ts) caps
# blast radius by tracking estimated daily spend per-IP and globally.
#
# When either ceiling is breached, /api/chat and /api/fast return 503 with
# a friendly "we're at capacity for today" message. Fast Check stays open
# (lower per-call cost) until the global ceiling itself is hit.
#
# Defaults: $5 global / $1 per-IP. Tune to your appetite.
# Set lower to be conservative; raise if real users keep tripping the cap.
# DAILY_SPEND_CEILING_USD=5
# PER_IP_DAILY_CEILING_USD=1

# ─── Recommended in production (waitlist email capture) ─────────────────────

# Supabase project that stores the waitlist (users who hit the daily
# spend ceiling and ask to be notified). When unset, the waitlist falls
# back to an in-memory list — fine for local dev but lost on redeploy in
# production.
#
# To set up: create a Supabase project (https://supabase.com), apply the
# create_waitlist_table migration in `web/supabase/migrations/` (or via
# the Supabase MCP), and copy the URL + publishable key here.
#
# The publishable key is sufficient — RLS on the waitlist table allows
# anon INSERTs but blocks SELECT/UPDATE/DELETE. Read the captured
# entries via the Supabase dashboard.
SUPABASE_URL=${SUPABASE_URL}
SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=${SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY}

# ─── Recommended in production (waitlist email notifications) ────────────────

# Resend (https://resend.com — free tier 100 emails/day) sends a notification
# email whenever someone submits the waitlist form. Without RESEND_API_KEY OR
# WAITLIST_NOTIFY_EMAIL, the lib silently no-ops — submissions still land in
# Supabase, you just won't be alerted in real time.
#
# WAITLIST_NOTIFY_EMAIL is the address that receives notifications (you).
# WAITLIST_FROM_EMAIL is optional — defaults to onboarding@resend.dev which
# only delivers to YOUR own Resend-registered email. Set this to a
# verified-domain address (e.g. noreply@yourdomain.com) once you've added
# the domain in Resend.
RESEND_API_KEY=${RESEND_API_KEY}
WAITLIST_NOTIFY_EMAIL=${WAITLIST_NOTIFY_EMAIL}
# WAITLIST_FROM_EMAIL=noreply@yourdomain.com

# ─── Optional ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Override the allowed Origin for /api/* requests. By default the
# middleware derives the allowed origin from the Host header, which is
# correct for the deployed app. Set this only if you're running behind a
# custom domain that needs an explicit allowlist.
# ALLOWED_ORIGIN=https://your-custom-domain.com

# Optional analytics. Browser analytics starts only after visitor consent.
# Server-side PostHog events/errors remain disabled unless explicitly enabled.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=${NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY}
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://eu.i.posthog.com
# POSTHOG_SERVER_ENABLED=false
